WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.270
Imagine traveling in a foreign country when
you learn the news of your father’s death.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.720
Now, imagine that death means you are now
at the helm of the British Empire.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.480
That is how Queen Elizabeth II discovered
she would no longer be a Princess, but instead

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.160
would be Queen of the most powerful empire
in history.

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.779
She was only 25 years old, and would soon
become the head of one of the world’s most

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:28.550
powerful monarchies; a post she would hold
for much of the 20th Century…

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.769
Elizabeth wasn’t even supposed to be Queen.

00:00:30.769 --> 00:00:41.329
But as the old saying goes, “when humans
plan, God laughs.”

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:44.480
Early Life

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.329
Elizabeth was born on April 21, 1926, to the
Duke and Duchess of York.

00:00:49.329 --> 00:00:53.739
Her father, Prince Albert, was at that time
second in line to the monarchy, following

00:00:53.739 --> 00:00:58.780
the current King, George V, and his older
brother, Edward, Prince of Wales.

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:03.079
And should her parents, or Prince Albert,
have had a son, the male heirs would jump

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:05.760
ahead of Elizabeth in the line of succession.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.220
It was Elizabeth’s Uncle Edward, the Prince
of Wales, whose behavior altered this line

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.220
of succession.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:16.420
In love with an American divorcee, Edward
abdicated the throne in 1936 amid a public

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.330
uproar about his love life...

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:23.259
Elizabeth’s father became King George VI,
and Elizabeth became next in line to the throne

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:25.290
at the age of just ten.

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:30.049
Reportedly, Elizabeth’s younger sister Margaret
told her “Poor you,” upon learning the

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:32.219
news of the new line of succession...

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:36.299
Before the family was thrust into the role
of monarchs, Elizabeth, Margaret, and their

00:01:36.299 --> 00:01:41.409
parents lived a relatively quiet life split
between homes in London and the countryside.

00:01:41.409 --> 00:01:44.450
Elizabeth was educated at home by a governess.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:49.130
She wasn’t around children her own age,
with the exception of her sister, a somewhat

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:52.840
lonely experience she tried to avoid for her
own children.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.479
During these lessons, Elizabeth enjoyed learning
about history and also showed herself to be

00:01:57.479 --> 00:02:00.090
a responsible, organized child.

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:05.340
She even insisted on properly setting up her
toy horses before bed each night, making sure

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:13.040
each was unsaddled and fed.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.810
As intelligent and responsible as she showed
herself to be, Elizabeth still showed herself

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:21.840
to be like many other children in one regard...

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.590
She had trouble pronouncing her own name.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.739
Instead of ‘Elizabeth’, she said ‘Lilibet.’

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:31.730
As a result, the nickname stuck, and it was
what her close family would come to know her

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:32.730
as.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.180
As Elizabeth grew older, and became next in
line to the throne, more emphasis was placed

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.709
on formal education.

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:41.720
As a 12-year-old, she was taught constitutional
history from a member of Eton’s faculty,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.580
and the Archbishop of Canterbury was her religion
instructor.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.050
It was not exactly a typically education...

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:55.489
But it wasn’t all dry, scholastic studies
- being the daughter of a King does have its

00:02:55.489 --> 00:02:56.489
perks, after all.

00:02:56.489 --> 00:03:00.650
To help increase Elizabeth’s social reach
and outdoors education, a Buckingham Palace

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.049
chapter of the Girl Guides was started.

00:03:03.049 --> 00:03:06.840
Each Wednesday afternoon, twenty fortunate
girls were welcomed to the palace to take

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:16.989
part in outdoors-oriented activities and be
a part of the future Queen’s social circle…

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:22.099
War

00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:26.760
But the comfortable, innocent childhood of
Elizabeth was not to last.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:32.310
Coming of age in the 1930s, her life would
be affected by World War Two before she turned

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.310
15.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:38.900
By 1939, England was at war, and though pressure
mounted to send Elizabeth and Margaret to

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:41.880
Canada, their mother would hear of no such
thing...

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.470
Instead, they were sent to live at Windsor
Castle in 1940.

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:50.230
This served two purposes - keeping Elizabeth
and Margaret safer than they might otherwise

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:55.420
be in London, but also helping to show the
strength of the country in a time of terrible

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.830
upheaval and fear.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:01.310
If England was safe enough for the princesses,
then it was safe enough for the rest of the

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:06.730
country too, and Brits should be proud and
patriotic to do their duty for the war, just

00:04:06.730 --> 00:04:09.260
like the young princesses were doing.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:13.400
While the royal sisters were staying in a
castle and were closely watched over, that

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.700
does not mean the princesses were completely
exempt from all of the fears and uncertainty

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:19.840
of war.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.890
While they were staying at Windsor, hundreds
of bombs were dropped in the area and like

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:28.610
so many of their countrymen, Elizabeth and
Margaret were roused from bed in the middle

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:33.450
of the night, rushed to bomb shelters, and
kept on constant alert for the air raid siren.

00:04:33.450 --> 00:04:38.420
In October of 1940, at the age of fourteen,
Elizabeth gave a radio address that was heard

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:40.820
throughout England and North America.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:46.730
She discussed the situation she and Margaret
were in, talking about how they understood

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.480
what their evacuated peers were experiencing...

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:54.060
being separated from their loved ones and
living in a world turned upside down by violence.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.820
But Elizabeth wanted to do more for her country
than speak on the radio...

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:02.940
She was not yet 18, and it wasn’t until
near the end of the war that she was given

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.510
the opportunity to serve as a trainee ambulance
driver.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:11.380
She learned about car repair, and photos of
her working became iconic images of the British

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:12.480
war effort.

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.140
On VE Day, she wore her uniform and stood
on the balcony with Prime Minister Churchill

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:18.450
and her parents.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:31.050
Elizabeth was not yet Queen, but even as a
teenager, she had shown herself to be a leader.

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.720
Marriage

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.840
The war ended in 1945, but its aftermath remained,
even for royalty.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.690
When Elizabeth married two years after the
end of the war, she purchased the fabric for

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.370
her wedding gown using ration coupons.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:50.550
But her dress was hardly a dreary affair;
it was made of silk and decorated with over

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.910
10,000 pearls.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.750
Elizabeth’s groom was Prince Philip of Greece,
a man who had been in and out of Elizabeth’s

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.900
life since she was only 13 years old.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:02.710
As second cousins, they saw each other at
family functions throughout their lives.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:07.181
Teenage Elizabeth had been intrigued by Philip
since meeting him in her early teens, but

00:06:07.181 --> 00:06:12.470
it was when Elizabeth was 17 and he 22 that
the Prince began to consider marrying her.

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:17.870
Now as we mentioned, they were second-cousins,
but such a marriage between cousins is hardly

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.350
unusual for royalty.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:23.610
Philip was born in Greece, and was a member
of the line of succession to the Greek throne.

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:28.250
However, he gave up his position in the line
of succession to become a naturalized British

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:29.250
citizen.

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.520
The night before their wedding he was made
the Duke of Edinburgh.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.740
The two announced their engagement in July
of 1947, celebrating the happy occasion with

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:38.060
a garden party.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:44.770
In photos from the party, Elizabeth is unabashedly
excited, while Philip gazes down at her adoringly.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:49.720
Only four months later, in November of 1947,
the two were married in a ceremony at Westminster

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:50.720
Abbey.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.420
The Archbishops of Canterbury and York performed
the rite, with royalty around the world in

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:56.420
attendance.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:07:00.500
200 million commoners were able to listen
in via a BBC radio broadcast.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.600
After the ceremony, Elizabeth and Philip,
with Elizabeth still wearing her wedding gown,

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.370
waved to cheering crowds from the balcony
of Buckingham Palace.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:13.590
Shortly after, Elizabeth gave birth to their
first child, Prince Charles, in 1948.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.800
A daughter, Anne, was added to the family
in 1950.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.740
It would be over a decade before Elizabeth
gave birth to her youngest sons, Prince Edward

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.110
and Prince Andrew.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.140
Interestingly: Elizabeth was serving as Queen
when Prince Edward and Prince Andrew were

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:34.220
born, making her the first Queen in England’s
history to give birth while holding the title!

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:41.910
Becoming Queen

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:46.000
It was her husband Philip who had the difficult
duty of passing the word to his wife that

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.160
her father had passed.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.580
The two knew he was ill; in fact they were
travelling in Kenya because the King was not

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:55.860
well enough to travel internationally so his
daughter and son-in-law took on the duty.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:01.060
The trip was supposed to include travel to
Australia and New Zealand, but of course news

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:04.150
of the King’s death took priority over all
else.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:08.990
Elizabeth, true to the stoic stereotype of
the British and the royals, reportedly did

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:13.690
not succumb to her emotions upon hearing the
news, but instead began planning for the practical

00:08:13.690 --> 00:08:15.699
steps that must come next...

00:08:15.699 --> 00:08:19.720
She and Philip returned to England, and it
was on the plane flight home that she finally

00:08:19.720 --> 00:08:23.550
allowed herself to cry - although never in
public, of course.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:29.190
Even then, she hid in the bathroom and emerged
with reddened eyes but a composed face.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:33.280
Years later, Elizabeth recalled the implications
of taking the throne at such a young age,

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.560
“In a way I didn’t have an apprenticeship.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:40.270
My father died much too young … It’s a
question of maturing into something that one’s

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:44.570
got used to doing and accepting the fact that
here you here, and it’s your fate, because

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.720
I think that continuity is very important.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.890
It is a job for life,” she said.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.490
Preparations for Elizabeth’s coronation
ceremony took over a year.

00:08:52.490 --> 00:08:57.520
Her father died in February of 1952, but the
official coronation did not occur until June

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:58.970
of 1953.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.830
It rained on the day of her coronation, but
this did nothing to temper the excitement

00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:06.270
of the day for the British people who had
watched this young woman grow up.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:10.830
Elizabeth was coronated in Westminster Abbey,
bringing her into the line of kings and queens

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.280
who had been crowned there for nearly a millennium.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.920
Not even thirty years old, Elizabeth solemnly
delivered the coronation oath which was heard

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:19.300
by millions around the world.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:23.550
The ceremony, in a sign of the changing times,
was the first British coronation ceremony

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:24.780
to be televised.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.560
In her coronation speech, Elizabeth looked
to the future, saying the following:

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:36.180
“Therefore I am sure that this, my Coronation,
is not the symbol of a power and a splendor

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:45.930
that are gone but a declaration of our hopes
for the future, and for the years I may, by

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:52.280
God's Grace and Mercy, be given to reign and
serve you as your Queen.”

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:56.110
Following the actual ceremony, Elizabeth and
dozens of other dignitaries rode through the

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:59.260
streets of London, cheered by adoring crowds.

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:03.900
Still more applauding Brits stood and cheered
her as she waved to them from the balcony

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:05.830
of Buckingham Palace later in the evening...

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:06.830
Elizabeth’s Duties

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.360
Elizabeth and Philip moved into Buckingham
Palace after the coronation, but they did

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:11.210
not spend much time there initially.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:15.400
Instead, they took off on a world tour for
months of 1953 and 1954.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:20.380
The tour was the brainchild of Prime Minister
Winston Churchill, who recognized the appeal

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:26.210
of Queen Elizabeth to the masses and the possibilities
such a favored Queen presented for building

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:28.460
a positive image for England abroad.

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:32.620
The royal couple went around the world, making
Elizabeth the first European monarch to do

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.620
so.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:38.590
She was also the first reigning British monarch
to visit Australia, New Zealand, and Fiji.

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.370
This trip was only the first of many Elizabeth
would take as Queen.

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:47.380
During her 65 years (and counting) as Queen,
she has become the world’s most widely-travelled

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:48.380
monarch.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.950
And, as Queen, she has an easier time than
the rest of us - because of the way British

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.900
and international law works, she doesn’t
actually need a passport to travel.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:00.040
If she did have a passport, though, it would
be plenty full.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:02.440
She has made nearly three hundred foreign
trips to 120 countries.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.440
Canada, the United States, New Zealand, Australia,
India, Bermuda, Iran, Saudi Arabia, Qatar,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:13.350
Thailand, Brazil, and Russia are only a 
few of the nations she has visited.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:17.510
Some of her most notable trips include the
opening Canada’s Parliament, been the honoree

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:22.890
at a ticker tape parade in New York City,
awarded Mother Teresa the Order of Merit in

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.710
India, and speaking to the United Nations.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:30.390
Travelling has been a large part of Queen
Elizabeth’s reign, but representing her

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:33.290
nation abroad is not her only duty...

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:38.190
As the monarch in a constitutional monarchy,
she is tasked with many domestic duties.

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:43.370
Though she does not get involved in politics,
she must stay well-informed of what is going

00:11:43.370 --> 00:11:45.820
on within her country, and around the world.

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:51.220
Each day, she receives piles of reading material
encased in red metal boxes.

00:11:51.220 --> 00:11:55.970
The tradition of these red boxes dates back
nearly two centuries, and until recently parliamentary

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.290
ministers also received them.

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:01.360
While Parliament has recently opted to switch
to digital delivery of information, the Queen

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:06.850
remains tied to paper delivery, so those red
boxes continue to arrive daily at her door.

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:11.770
The material contained within encompasses
everything from foreign intelligence to economic

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:15.390
updates to the most mundane matters of state.

00:12:15.390 --> 00:12:19.960
Reading and digesting the information can
take several hours a day, but it is a crucial

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.980
part of her role as a leader.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:26.350
Even today, at the age of ninety, she receives
her red box each day, and takes the time to

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:29.300
go through the crucial information contained
within.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:33.680
Queen Elizabeth’s more public duties include
presenting medals, issuing knighthoods, and

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:38.390
presiding over many ceremonies that take place
in a society that still has a monarch.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:42.350
As she has aged, some of Elizabeth’s duties
have been passed down to her children and

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:50.580
grandchildren, particularly ceremonies where
the nobility receive their titles.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:54.940
Growing Family

00:12:54.940 --> 00:13:00.810
Over the years, Elizabeth’s family grew
to include grandchildren and great-grandchildren.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:06.360
Her family, despite their royal blood, have
not been immune from the ups and downs of

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:07.960
life and love…

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:13.000
Three of her four children have been through
divorces with the world watching, and the

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:17.720
death of Princess Diana shook the entire family,
and the world.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:23.340
It was 1992 when much of the royal family
seemed to crumble, with the marriages of Princess

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:28.600
Anne, Prince Charles, and Prince Andrew all
coming to their ends in one way or another.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:33.480
It was also the year that Windsor Castle caught
on fire, and the public became outraged at

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:38.440
the idea their tax money would be used to
rebuild it when the Queen was one of the richest

00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:40.029
women in the world.

00:13:40.029 --> 00:13:43.230
That year, though, the Queen demonstrated
her political acumen when it was announced

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:48.520
that the royal family would pay for the restoration
of Windsor Castle, and, in a reversal of a

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:55.050
thousand year old policy, the family would
begin paying taxes.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:14:02.029
Now, Queen Elizabeth had long understand the
importance of public opinion.

00:14:02.029 --> 00:14:06.100
She was the power behind the British monarchy’s
increased visibility to the media.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:10.480
She recognized that the public wanted to know
more about the family and feel like they knew

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:11.520
the royals.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:15.070
Increased transparency was one way to help
the royal family connect with the public,

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:17.820
and it has long been a hallmark of Elizabeth’s
reign.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:22.640
Hers was the first televised coronation, and
then she was the first to allow television

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.600
cameras into Buckingham Palace.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:28.300
Live cameras were allowed into the Palace
during President Nixon’s visit; an informal

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:32.800
event that included lunch and the swapping
of signed photographs between the world leaders.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.820
Elizabeth also welcomed cameras into the family’s
lives for the production of a documentary

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:41.620
on the royals, hoping to show them as simply
humans who loved their family members...

00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:46.020
But it was in 2007 that Buckingham Palace
really got with the times and launched an

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:47.670
official YouTube Channel.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:52.160
This was ten years after perhaps one of the
most important displays of Elizabeth use the

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:56.600
media to effectively communicate with the
public - her speech about Princess Diana…

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:07.180
The Death of Diana

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:11.460
When Princess Diana died, amid the public
mourning and the private grief of the family,

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:16.130
Elizabeth broke protocol, recognising that
the country, and the world, needed to hear

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:17.130
from the royal family.

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:21.560
Queen Elizabeth II and Princess Diana were
initially close, and Diana even called her

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:26.180
‘mama,’ but, over time, the relationship
became, at best, strained.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:31.310
Diana regularly broke royal protocol, and
aired the dirty laundry of her and Charles’

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:32.310
personal lives.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:38.250
The Queen, a stoic monarch from a different
generation did not understand Diana’s desire

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:43.529
to be involved with AIDs charities or her
willingness to talk publicly about the disintegration

00:15:43.529 --> 00:15:44.589
of her marriage.

00:15:44.589 --> 00:15:49.040
To this day, there are people like Diana’s
lover, Dodi Fayed’s father who blame the

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:53.390
Queen and the royal family for the car crash
that killed Diana and his son.

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:57.580
When Queen Elizabeth and her family did not
immediately express their grief publicly the

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:01.240
press and the people of the world were outraged.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:05.580
Eventually though, Queen Elizabeth did give
a live address speaking about Diana and the

00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:07.010
tragedy of her death.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:10.339
The Queen spoke of Diana in the way most of
the world viewed her:

00:16:10.339 --> 00:16:14.370
“First, I want to pay tribute to Diana myself.

00:16:14.370 --> 00:16:18.980
She was an exceptional and gifted human being.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:24.130
In good times and bad, she never lost her
capacity to smile and laugh, nor to inspire

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:25.260
others with her warmth and kindness.

00:16:25.260 --> 00:16:36.871
I admired and respected her -- for her energy
and commitment to others, and especially for

00:16:36.871 --> 00:16:37.871
her devotion to her two boys.”

00:16:37.871 --> 00:16:41.360
While public opinion didn’t immediately
fully rebound in the Queen’s favor, the

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:48.970
address was helpful in stemming the tide of
criticism.

00:16:48.970 --> 00:16:54.580
Later Life

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.580
Unlike her children, Queen Elizabeth has had
a long marriage.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:01.070
She and Philip have the longest marriage of
anyone in the royal family.

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:04.780
2017 marking their seventieth wedding anniversary.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:09.589
Their major wedding anniversaries have long
been celebrated in England, with their twenty-fifth

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:14.140
marked by a party that included other couples
from around England who were also married

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:17.130
on November 20th, 1947.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:21.420
In 2007, the couple sentimentally returned
to their honeymoon site in the Hampshire region

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:22.420
of England.

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:27.459
There, like any commoner couple might do,
they recreated their honeymoon photos.

00:17:27.459 --> 00:17:35.360
At their fiftieth anniversary, Queen Elizabeth,
in what is regarded as an emotional expression

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:40.620
for a member of the British Monarchy, said
this of her husband and her marriage:

00:17:40.620 --> 00:17:45.830
“He is someone who doesn't take easily to
compliments but he has, quite simply, been

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:53.180
my strength and stay all these years, and
I, and his whole family, and this and many

00:17:53.180 --> 00:17:59.290
other countries, owe him a debt greater than
he would ever claim, or we shall ever know.”

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:04.400
Queen Elizabeth’s reign has also been punctuated
by celebrations of milestones not just in

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:07.500
her marriage, but also in the number of her
reigning years.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:14.210
Her silver jubilee, celebrating 25 years of
her reign in 1977, was a nationwide party,

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:18.390
marked by sporting events, carnivals, concerts
and general festivities.

00:18:18.390 --> 00:18:23.929
Twenty-five years later, in 2002, her golden
jubilee was just as merry an affair, even

00:18:23.929 --> 00:18:27.360
though both her mother and sister had died
earlier in the year.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:31.050
To mark this jubilee, Queen Elizabeth and
Prince Philip travelled around the world.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:34.480
In June, a jubilee weekend was held to mark
the fifty years.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.640
One event, called “The Party at the Palace,”
particularly stood out.

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:42.340
Paul McCartney and Eric Clapton performed,
and Brian May played a version of God Save

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:43.340
the Queen.

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:48.430
It was a raucous celebration, filled with
some of Britain’s most recognizable celebrities,

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:51.000
and it was watched around the world.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:55.059
Thousands crowded London’s parks and streets
to see their favorite performers, sing along

00:18:55.059 --> 00:19:03.350
with their favorite songs, and most of all
to cheer on the Queen.

00:19:03.350 --> 00:19:08.680
The Queen Today

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:15.390
Though stoic and dutiful, Queen Elizabeth
II has set herself apart as a modern monarch.

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:20.280
She is well-known to be an animal lover, and
her corgis just a recognizable part of the

00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:24.160
royal family (perhaps as much as its human
members).

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:29.150
During her reign, she has had over thirty
corgis as pets, and they have even been involved

00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:33.340
in public affairs such as the opening ceremonies
for the London Olympics.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:37.300
The corgis haven’t always been as cute and
cuddly as they look, though.

00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:42.040
At one point the royal family reportedly had
to bring in an animal psychologist to help

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:47.150
improve the dogs’ behavior when they started
nipping at the many people who were around

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:48.410
the royal residences.

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:53.010
The Queen herself was even injured once when
she found herself in the midst of ten fighting

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:54.030
corgis!

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:57.870
These incidents did nothing to temper her
love for the animals though, and the Queen’s

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:02.580
corgis will forever be remembered, as they
are included with Elizabeth’s image on such

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:06.260
items as the golden jubilee commemorative
coins.

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:10.090
Apart from owning and pampering corgis, Queen
Elizabeth has shown herself to be an animal

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.610
lover through her support of charities.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:17.270
Among the charities she supports are over
four dozen that support animals in need.

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:22.110
She is also generous in her donations to medical
charities, giving to nearly 100 of them.

00:20:22.110 --> 00:20:24.470
She also donates money to her church.

00:20:24.470 --> 00:20:29.410
Interestingly, she takes the money to church
in her purse, making Sunday the only day of

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.230
the week when she carries money.

00:20:31.230 --> 00:20:35.361
Overall, it’s estimated that Queen Elizabeth’s
charitable actions have resulted in over one

00:20:35.361 --> 00:20:45.380
billion dollars being donated to charity…

00:20:45.380 --> 00:20:49.380
Queen Elizabeth II has been in the public
eye since childhood.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:54.650
Now in her nineties, and her sixty-fifth year
as Queen, she was a world leader for much

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:58.200
of the 20th Century and has helped usher in
the 21st.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:02.150
Her reign has spanned that of twelve U.S.
Presidents, twelve U.K.

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:06.429
Prime Ministers, seven Popes, and six Archbishops
of Canterbury.

00:21:06.429 --> 00:21:12.110
She has replied to more than 3.5 million pieces
of correspondence, and received thousands

00:21:12.110 --> 00:21:14.870
of birthday greetings throughout her lifetime.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:19.040
She has met with leaders from all corners
of the world, and surpassed longevity records

00:21:19.040 --> 00:21:21.230
of both British and foreign monarchs.

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:25.559
She is one of only five British monarchs who
have ruled for more than fifty years, and

00:21:25.559 --> 00:21:28.970
in 2015 she became the longest-ever reigning
British monarch.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:32.670
Previously, that title had been held by Queen
Victoria, Elizabeth’s great-great-grandmother,

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:35.809
who reigned for sixty-three years.

00:21:35.809 --> 00:21:40.000
Queen Elizabeth II is an icon of the twentieth
and twenty-first centuries.

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:45.180
A strong female leader, who retains a love
of her people and a strong standing in the

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:46.210
international community.

00:21:46.210 --> 00:21:50.360
She helped bring the monarchy into the modern
world, and remained stoic and dutiful through

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:53.230
even the most trying of times.

00:21:53.230 --> 00:22:02.519
God Save the Queen.

